<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Segovia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Segovia is a city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and León in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, on a ridge north of the Guadarrama mountains, and capital of the province of the same name. Originally a Celtiberian settlement, it grew under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, whose great aqueduct, probably built around the end of the 1st century CE, still crosses the city. The old town and aqueduct are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/segovia.jpg" alt="Segovia" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carlos Delgado · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Segovia_-_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Segovia is the capital and most populated municipality of the Province of Segovia, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and León. It lies on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meseta-central">Inner Plateau</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a>, near the northern slopes of the Sistema Central, with its housing set on a bend of the Eresma river. The main route of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camino-de-santiago">Camino de Santiago</a> de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> passes through it. Its name is of Celtiberian origin and is mentioned by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> in the context of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sertorian-war">Sertorian War</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first recorded settlement on the site was a Celtic possession, which later passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>. The city is a possible site of the battle of 75 BCE in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-caecilius-metellus-pius">Quintus Caecilius Metellus Pius</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-sertorius">Quintus Sertorius</a> and Hirtuleius. After Alfonso VI of León and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>, Segovia was resettled with Christians from the north of the peninsula and from beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a>.</p><p>Its position on trade routes made it an important centre of the wool and textile trade, and the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> was something of a golden age, with a growing Jewish population. Isabella I was proclaimed queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> in Segovia on 13 December 1474. The city joined the Revolt of the Comuneros under Juan Bravo, and its population rose to 27,000 in 1594, then fell to only 8,000 by 1694 as Castilian cities declined. The Royal School of Artillery, the first military academy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, opened in 1764. French troops sacked the city in 1808.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The old city and its aqueduct were declared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Sites</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> in 1985. The Roman aqueduct, dating from the late 1st or early 2nd century, consists of about 25,000 granite blocks held together without mortar and spans 818 metres with more than 170 arches. The Alcázar, a royal palace on a stone peninsula between the Eresma and Clamores, is first documented in 1122 and was rebuilt after a fire in 1862. The cathedral, consecrated in 1768, is the last Gothic cathedral built in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. The walls, enlarged by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI</a>, encircle the historic quarter.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Segovia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Roman engineering left lasting monuments: the aqueduct of <b>Segovia</b>, the Tower of Hercules lighthouse at A Coruña, still in use, the walls of Lugo, the gold-mining landscape of Las Médulas and the ruins of Tarraco are all UNESCO World Heritage Sites.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hispania-tarraconensis">Hispania Tarraconensis · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In the bull Vineae Domini custodes, Pope Honorius III approved a mission to Morocco, then part of the Almohad empire, asked for by the friars Dominic of <b>Segovia</b> and Martin.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-10">10 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Arevaci occupied the upper Douro basin in modern Soria and <b>Segovia</b> from about the 4th century BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/arevaci">Arevaci</a></li></ul></section>
